 important for that patient survival and  hopefully that will lead to personalized  medicine down the road but one way that  we're using this method is to try to  understand what's causing these tumor  suppressors the loss of these tumor  suppressors make this prostate cancer so  aggressive and so unable to be treated  this is just an example of an organizer  that eyes and growing in the lab this is  from a mouse tumor and you can see here  that there's webbing here this is  actually an organized that was live so  you can't see the cell but it's growing  here both in all directions is growing  and we're using this method because it  takes about three weeks to grow some  organized in the lab however when we use  a mouse model that can take years to  actually get to a point where there's a  tumor that we can test drugs for for and  so eventually we can use this method to  test many different types of drugs much  faster so they can get to patients and  finally I just like to discuss we're  currently ongoing doing experiments with  the organoid I just wanted to show you a  little bit from a beautiful paper in  cancer cell Blattner at all that was  published earlier this year and this is  a type of experiment that we are doing  so you can see here that these are  organized that they grew and you can see  that they form gland like structures  like I showed you earlier in the  presentation  there's gland like structures here with  cells on the outside and pink protein on  the inside that pink is actually the  protein cells are are purple because  that stains the DNA and you can also use  this you basically cut the organize  imagine that organizers are cut directly  in half and you can see them saying  different things like I showed you  previously when we say in the tumor and  you can also do oh they're beautiful  staining with some fluorescent markers  or you can say many different things at  one time and so we are pursuing many  different avenues using the organize  that